    "q-72344": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72344",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14131515/assets/4066b07ace047e9a7ce813ed70dd6648649a192d648ed810fa96b5146d2c5581.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type II region in two-dimensional space?",
      "worked_solution": "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x-1}\\,⟹\\,x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1\n$$\n\nThe function $x=0$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=e.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le e.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14131515/assets/680842905f6e308d3c438063769bde9854401a300371452d1c0735bb7d272a02.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=0$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le e,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)+1\\}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
